-- title:
--   Nilpotence of the Hasse–Witt matrix of [p]_F
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $\mathcal O$ a commutative ring in which $p$ is a non-zero-divisor, equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure on $\mathbb Z/p$ whose structure map has kernel exactly the ideal $(p)$, and assume $\mathcal O$ is adically complete for $(p)$. Let $F$ be a $d$-dimensional formal group law over $\mathcal O$, that is, a $d$-tuple $F_i \in \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d,Y_1,\dots,Y_d]]$ with vanishing constant term, linear part $X_i+Y_i$ and the associativity identity, and assume $F$ is commutative (invariant under interchanging the two blocks of variables). Let $v \ge 1$, and let $R$ be a commutative ring which is a Hopf algebra over $\mathcal O$, free and finite as an $\mathcal O$-module. Let $\pi \colon \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d]] \to R$ be a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map such that each $\pi(X_i)$ lies in the radical of $(p)R$, such that $\pi$ is computed as $(p)$-adic evaluation at the elements $\pi(X_i)$ (the `adicEval` of the series), such that $\ker \pi$ is generated by the $d$ components of the $p^v$-fold iterate `F.nthSeries (p ^ v)` (the series $[p^v]_F$, defined by $[0]_F=0$ and $[n+1]_F=F([n]_F(X),X)$), and such that for each $i$ the comultiplication satisfies $\mathrm{comul}(\pi(X_i)) = F_i$ evaluated $(p)$-adically in $R \otimes_{\mathcal O} R$ at the $\pi(X_j)\otimes 1$ and $1 \otimes \pi(X_j)$. Assume finally that the Cartier dual of $\mathbb Z/p \otimes_{\mathcal O} R$, namely the $\mathbb Z/p$-linear dual `Module.Dual` of $\mathbb Z/p \otimes_{\mathcal O} R$ with its convolution ring structure, is a local ring. Then the $d \times d$ matrix over $\mathbb Z/p$ whose $(i,j)$ entry is the image in $\mathbb Z/p$ of the coefficient of $X_j^p$ in the $i$-th component of $[p]_F$ is nilpotent.
--
--   This is the implication 'unipotent $p^v$-torsion $\Rightarrow$ nilpotent Verschiebung on the tangent space' in the form: the Hasse–Witt matrix of $[p]_F$ modulo $p$, i.e. the matrix of $X_j^p$-coefficients, is nilpotent; in dimension one it says that the height of $F$ is at least $2$. It is used in the analysis of $p$-divisible groups attached to formal groups, feeding into [`MvFormalGroup.exists_rescaledExp_tendsto_zero_of_isLocalRing_cartierDual_of_eq_two`](thm.html#MvFormalGroup.exists_rescaledExp_tendsto_zero_of_isLocalRing_cartierDual_of_eq_two) and into a coefficient estimate in the deformation-theoretic part of the development.
-- source:
